From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:47:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: samsung: Add Exynos9820 pin controller support

Exynos9820 has eight pin controllers, ALIVE, AUD, CMGP, FSYS0, FSYS1,
PERIC0, PERIC1 and VTS, with 64 banks and 234 pins between them. The
driver keeps that geometry in static tables rather than in the device
tree, so without them the samsung,exynos9820-pinctrl compatible has
nothing to bind to: none of the eight controllers probe, and with them
go every pin configuration and every GPIO on the SoC.

The bank names, pin counts, register offsets and EINT offsets are a
transcription of the vendor tables. Both bank types the vendor uses are
field for field ones this driver already has. Its non-alive type is
{ 4, 1, 4, 4, 2, 4 } at offsets { 0x00, 0x04, 0x08, 0x0c, 0x10, 0x14 },
which is exynos850_bank_type_off, and its alive type is { 4, 1, 4, 4 }
at { 0x00, 0x04, 0x08, 0x0c }, which is exynos850_bank_type_alive. The
vendor bank macro carries one argument more, a filter control offset.
struct samsung_pin_bank_data does have a field for it, added for
gs101, but the EXYNOS850_PIN_BANK_* macros do not set it, so the
vendor's value is dropped here.

Which controller gets which callback does not follow from the bank
types and is taken from the vendor as well. AUD and VTS get none: both
sit behind their own PMU power switch, AUD_CONFIGURATION and
VTS_CONFIGURATION, so their registers cannot be touched at boot the way
the other six can.

The order of the tables is the order of the pinctrl0..pinctrl7 aliases
that the device tree later in this series declares, because
samsung_pinctrl_get_soc_data() indexes the table by that alias.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
